var e={level:`B2`,type:`sentence-choice`,questions:[{id:`b2-sc-001`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In a relative clause the relative pronoun is the object, so do not repeat it with it.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Do not repeat the object: 'which' already replaces 'it'.`,2:`'what' cannot be used as a relative pronoun after a noun.`},options:[`The book which I bought it was boring.`,`The book which I bought was boring.`,`The book what I bought was boring.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-002`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`be-used-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Be used to' is followed by the -ing form: I'm used to getting up early.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'be used to', use the -ing form.`,2:`'I used to' is followed by the base form and means a past habit.`},options:[`I'm used to getting up early.`,`I'm used to get up early.`,`I used to getting up early.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-003`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The third conditional uses if + past perfect and would have + past participle: If I had known, I would have left earlier.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Do not use 'would' in the if-clause; use the past perfect 'had known'.`,2:`After 'had' you need the past participle 'known', not the base form 'know'.`,3:`The result of a third conditional needs 'would have', not 'will have'.`},options:[`If I would have known about the traffic, I would have left earlier.`,`If I had known about the traffic, I would have left earlier.`,`If I had know about the traffic, I would have left earlier.`,`If I had known about the traffic, I will have left earlier.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-004`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Tell' needs a person as object, but 'say' does not: She told me that... / She said that...`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Say' cannot take a person directly as object; use 'told me' or 'said to me'.`,2:`'Tell' must be followed by the person you speak to, for example 'told me'.`,3:`Do not put 'to' after 'tell'; the person follows the verb directly.`},options:[`She told me that she was feeling tired.`,`She said me that she was feeling tired.`,`She told that she was feeling tired.`,`She told to me that she was feeling tired.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-005`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The present continuous passive is am/is/are + being + past participle: The bridge is being repaired.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The bridge does not repair itself, so the verb must be passive: 'is being repaired'.`,1:`The continuous passive needs 'being' after 'is', not 'been'.`,2:`'Has' is followed by 'been', and the moment now needs 'is being repaired'.`},options:[`The bridge is repairing at the moment.`,`The bridge is been repaired at the moment.`,`The bridge has being repaired at the moment.`,`The bridge is being repaired at the moment.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-006`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Relative clauses between commas (extra information) use 'who' for people and never 'that': My sister, who lives in Berlin, ...`,wrongReasons:{0:`'That' cannot introduce a relative clause between commas.`,1:`'Which' is for things; for a person use 'who'.`,3:`'Whom' is an object pronoun; here the pronoun is the subject, so use 'who'.`},options:[`My sister, that lives in Berlin, is an architect.`,`My sister, which lives in Berlin, is an architect.`,`My sister, who lives in Berlin, is an architect.`,`My sister, whom lives in Berlin, is an architect.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-007`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`To talk about a present situation we want to be different, use wish + past simple: I wish I had more free time.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'wish' for the present, move the verb back to the past: 'had'.`,2:`We do not use 'would' after 'I wish I...'; use the past simple.`,3:`'Wish' is not followed by 'will'; for a future hope use 'hope'.`},options:[`I wish I have more free time.`,`I wish I had more free time.`,`I wish I would have more free time.`,`I wish I will have more free time.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-008`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Must have + past participle' expresses a strong deduction about the past: She must have forgotten.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'must' use the base form; for the past you need 'must have forgotten'.`,2:`Modals are followed by the base form 'have', never 'has'.`,3:`Positive deductions about the past use 'must have', not 'can have'.`},options:[`She must have forgotten about the meeting; she never misses one.`,`She must forgot about the meeting; she never misses one.`,`She must has forgotten about the meeting; she never misses one.`,`She can have forgotten about the meeting; she never misses one.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-009`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so it is followed by the -ing form: I'm looking forward to seeing you.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Here 'to' is a preposition, so it needs the -ing form 'seeing'.`,1:`The correct preposition after 'look forward' is 'to', not 'for'.`,2:`A future event needs the simple -ing form 'seeing', not 'have seen'.`},options:[`I'm looking forward to see you next month.`,`I'm looking forward for seeing you next month.`,`I'm looking forward to have seen you next month.`,`I'm looking forward to seeing you next month.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-010`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use the past perfect for an action that happened before another past action: When I arrived, the train had already left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The story is in the past, so the earlier action needs the past perfect 'had left'.`,1:`After 'had' use the past participle 'left'.`,3:`'Went' is the past simple; the past participle is 'gone'.`},options:[`When I arrived at the station, the train has already left.`,`When I arrived at the station, the train had already leave.`,`When I arrived at the station, the train had already left.`,`When I arrived at the station, the train had already went.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-011`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The future continuous (will be + -ing) describes an action in progress at a future time: This time next week I'll be lying on a beach.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'will' use the base form 'be', not 'being'.`,2:`'Will' is a modal and is not followed by 'to'.`,3:`The future continuous needs the -ing form 'lying'.`},options:[`This time next week, I'll being lying on a beach in Greece.`,`This time next week, I'll be lying on a beach in Greece.`,`This time next week, I will to be lying on a beach in Greece.`,`This time next week, I'll be lie on a beach in Greece.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-012`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Despite' and 'in spite of' are followed by a noun or -ing form, without 'of' after 'despite': Despite the rain, we enjoyed it.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Despite' is never followed by 'of'.`,2:`'Although' must be followed by a clause, such as 'it was raining'.`,3:`The full expression is 'in spite of', so 'of' is missing.`},options:[`Despite the rain, we enjoyed the match.`,`Despite of the rain, we enjoyed the match.`,`Although the rain, we enjoyed the match.`,`In spite the rain, we enjoyed the match.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-013`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The second conditional uses if + past simple and would + base form for unreal present situations: If she studied harder, she would pass.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Would' does not normally go in the if-clause of a second conditional.`,1:`Present 'studies' belongs to the first conditional; it cannot be followed by 'would pass'.`,2:`With the past simple in the if-clause, the result needs 'would', not 'will'.`},options:[`If she would study harder, she would pass the exam.`,`If she studies harder, she would pass the exam.`,`If she studied harder, she will pass the exam.`,`If she studied harder, she would pass the exam.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-014`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Reported questions use normal statement word order, with no auxiliary 'do': He asked me where I lived.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In a reported question we do not use 'did' or question word order.`,1:`Reported questions do not keep the question form 'do I live'; also the tense moves back to 'lived'.`,3:`'Ask' takes the person directly as object, without 'to'.`},options:[`He asked me where did I live.`,`He asked me where do I live.`,`He asked me where I lived.`,`He asked to me where I lived.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-015`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The future passive is will + be + past participle: The results will be announced next week.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The future passive needs 'be' between 'will' and the past participle.`,2:`After 'be' in the passive, use the past participle 'announced'.`,3:`After 'will' use the base form 'be', not 'being'.`},options:[`The results will announced next week.`,`The results will be announced next week.`,`The results will be announce next week.`,`The results will being announced next week.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-016`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Whose' shows possession in relative clauses: the man whose dog bit my son.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Who's' means 'who is'; possession needs 'whose'.`,2:`Do not use 'that his'; one word, 'whose', shows possession.`,3:`'Which' does not show possession and is not used for people.`},options:[`That's the man whose dog bit my son.`,`That's the man who's dog bit my son.`,`That's the man that his dog bit my son.`,`That's the man which dog bit my son.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-017`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`To regret something in the past, use wish + past perfect: She wishes she hadn't sold her guitar.`,wrongReasons:{0:`A past regret needs the past perfect 'hadn't sold', not the past simple.`,1:`'Would have' is not used after 'wish' for past regrets.`,2:`The present perfect is not used after 'wish'; use 'hadn't sold'.`},options:[`She wishes she didn't sell her old guitar.`,`She wishes she wouldn't have sold her old guitar.`,`She wishes she hasn't sold her old guitar.`,`She wishes she hadn't sold her old guitar.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-018`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Can't' expresses a negative deduction (it is impossible): He can't be at home; I saw him at the gym.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Mustn't' means something is forbidden; for impossibility use 'can't'.`,1:`Do not use 'to' after the modal 'can't'.`,3:`Modals do not use the auxiliary 'do' to make negatives.`},options:[`He mustn't be at home; I saw him at the gym five minutes ago.`,`He can't to be at home; I saw him at the gym five minutes ago.`,`He can't be at home; I saw him at the gym five minutes ago.`,`He doesn't must be at home; I saw him at the gym five minutes ago.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-019`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Avoid' is followed by the -ing form: She avoided answering the question.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Avoid' is never followed by 'to' + infinitive.`,2:`The base form cannot follow 'avoid'; use 'answering'.`,3:`There is no 'to' after 'avoid'; just use the -ing form.`},options:[`She avoided to answer the question.`,`She avoided answering the question.`,`She avoided answer the question.`,`She avoided to answering the question.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-020`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for a longer background action and the past simple for a short action that interrupts it: While she was cooking, the phone rang.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Was' needs '-ing' or a past participle; the short interruption is just 'rang'.`,2:`The present perfect continuous does not fit a story in the past; use 'was cooking'.`,3:`The continuous form needs the auxiliary 'was'.`},options:[`While she was cooking dinner, the phone rang.`,`While she was cooking dinner, the phone was ring.`,`While she has been cooking dinner, the phone rang.`,`While she cooking dinner, the phone rang.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-021`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The future perfect (will have + past participle) shows an action complete before a future time: By the end of the year we will have saved enough.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The word order is wrong; the future perfect is 'will have saved'.`,1:`After 'have' use the past participle 'saved'.`,2:`'Are having saved' is not a tense; use 'will have saved'.`},options:[`By the end of the year, we will save have enough money for a car.`,`By the end of the year, we will have save enough money for a car.`,`By the end of the year, we are having saved enough money for a car.`,`By the end of the year, we will have saved enough money for a car.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-022`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Although' joins two clauses by itself, so we do not add 'but': Although he was tired, he kept working.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Use either 'although' or 'but', never both in the same sentence.`,1:`'Despite' is followed by a noun or -ing form, not a full clause.`,3:`To join a contrasting clause use 'even though', not 'even' alone.`},options:[`Although he was tired, but he kept working.`,`Despite he was tired, he kept working.`,`Although he was tired, he kept working.`,`Even he was tired, he kept working.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-023`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not', so the verb after it is positive and in the present: Unless it rains, we will go out.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Do not use 'will' after 'unless', and do not add a negative because 'unless' is already negative.`,2:`'Unless' already means 'if not', so 'doesn't' creates a double negative.`,3:`After 'unless' we use the present simple to talk about the future, not 'will'.`},options:[`Unless it won't rain, we will have the picnic in the park.`,`Unless it rains, we will have the picnic in the park.`,`Unless it doesn't rain, we will have the picnic in the park.`,`Unless it will rain, we will have the picnic in the park.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-024`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Reported negative commands use tell + person + not to + base form: The teacher told us not to use our phones.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'to' we need the base form, and 'not' normally comes before 'to'.`,2:`Imperatives like 'don't use' change to 'not to use' in reported speech.`,3:`'Say' cannot be followed directly by the person; use 'told us'.`},options:[`The teacher told us not to use our phones.`,`The teacher told us to not using our phones.`,`The teacher told us don't use our phones.`,`The teacher said us not to use our phones.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-025`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`We can say 'subject + is said to + infinitive' to report general opinions: She is said to be the best surgeon.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The pattern 'is said' is followed by 'to' + infinitive, not the -ing form.`,1:`This must be passive ('is said'), because other people say it about her.`,2:`With a personal subject use 'is said to be'; 'It is said that...' needs 'It' as subject.`},options:[`She is said being the best surgeon in the city.`,`She says to be the best surgeon in the city.`,`She is said that she is the best surgeon in the city.`,`She is said to be the best surgeon in the city.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-026`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Where' replaces 'in which', so no preposition is needed at the end: the town where I grew up.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Where' already includes the meaning of 'in', so the final 'in' is extra.`,1:`With 'which' you need a preposition: 'which I grew up in' or 'in which I grew up'.`,3:`Do not put a preposition before 'where'; say 'in which' or just 'where'.`},options:[`This is the town where I grew up in.`,`This is the town which I grew up.`,`This is the town where I grew up.`,`This is the town in where I grew up.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-027`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Wish + would' expresses annoyance at someone else's behaviour: I wish you would stop interrupting me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Will' is not used after 'wish'; to complain about behaviour use 'would'.`,2:`After 'wish' the present simple 'stop' is wrong; use 'would stop'.`,3:`After 'would' use the base form 'stop'.`},options:[`I wish you will stop interrupting me.`,`I wish you would stop interrupting me.`,`I wish you stop interrupting me.`,`I wish you would stopping interrupting me.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-028`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Needn't have + past participle' means you did something that was not necessary: You needn't have bought milk.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The modal 'needn't' is followed directly by 'have', without 'to'.`,2:`'Didn't need' must be followed by 'to' + base form, e.g. 'didn't need to buy'.`,3:`After a modal use 'have', not 'had'.`},options:[`You needn't have bought any milk; we already had some.`,`You needn't to have bought any milk; we already had some.`,`You didn't need have bought any milk; we already had some.`,`You needn't had bought any milk; we already had some.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-029`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Manage' is followed by 'to' + infinitive: He managed to fix the computer.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Manage' takes 'to' + infinitive, not the -ing form.`,1:`'Manage' needs 'to' before the verb.`,2:`After the infinitive 'to' use the base form 'fix'.`},options:[`He managed fixing the computer by himself.`,`He managed fix the computer by himself.`,`He managed to fixing the computer by himself.`,`He managed to fix the computer by himself.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-030`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows how long an action had continued before a past moment: They had been walking for hours when they found a café.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Have been walking' is a present tense and does not match 'found'; use 'had been walking'.`,1:`After 'had been' the verb needs the -ing form 'walking'.`,3:`'Were been' is not a tense; use 'had been walking'.`},options:[`They have been walking for hours when they finally found a café.`,`They had been walk for hours when they finally found a café.`,`They had been walking for hours when they finally found a café.`,`They were been walking for hours when they finally found a café.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-031`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' for predictions based on present evidence: Look at those clouds! It's going to rain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'will' use the base form 'rain', and evidence suggests 'going to'.`,2:`After 'going to' use the base form 'rain'.`,3:`The structure needs '-ing': 'It's going to rain'.`},options:[`Look at those dark clouds! It will raining.`,`Look at those dark clouds! It's going to rain.`,`Look at those dark clouds! It's going to raining.`,`Look at those dark clouds! It's go to rain.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-032`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'So that' + subject + could/would expresses purpose in the past: She took a taxi so that she wouldn't be late.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The expression is 'so as not to be late'; 'so as' is not followed by a clause.`,2:`Purpose is not expressed with 'for not to'; use 'so as not to' or 'so that'.`,3:`'In order that' needs a full clause with a subject and verb.`},options:[`She took a taxi so that she wouldn't be late.`,`She took a taxi so as she wouldn't be late.`,`She took a taxi for not to be late.`,`She took a taxi in order that not be late.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-033`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`To ask about an imaginary situation, use would + base form and if + past simple: What would you do if you won?`,wrongReasons:{0:`The two clauses are reversed: 'would' should not be in the if-clause, and 'will' does not match an unreal situation.`,1:`'Will' is not used in the if-clause; the second conditional needs the past simple 'won'.`,2:`'Had' must be followed by a past participle ('won'), and here the simple past 'won' is needed anyway.`},options:[`What will you do if you would win the lottery?`,`What would you do if you will win the lottery?`,`What would you do if you had win the lottery?`,`What would you do if you won the lottery?`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-034`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Reported yes/no questions use 'if' or 'whether' + statement word order: She asked if I had finished.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'if' use statement word order: 'if I had finished'.`,1:`A reported yes/no question is introduced by 'if' or 'whether', not 'that'.`,3:`Reported questions need 'if/whether', statement order and a backshifted tense.`},options:[`She asked if had I finished the report.`,`She asked that I had finished the report.`,`She asked if I had finished the report.`,`She asked me have I finished the report.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-035`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Have something done' means someone else does a job for you: I had my car serviced.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In 'have something done' the verb must be a past participle: 'serviced'.`,2:`Do not use 'to be' in this pattern; just 'had my car serviced'.`,3:`'Last week' is finished past time, so use 'had', not 'have'.`},options:[`I had my car service last week.`,`I had my car serviced last week.`,`I had my car to be serviced last week.`,`I have my car serviced last week.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-036`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In formal English a preposition can go before 'whom': the scientist to whom the prize was given.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The verb needs 'to' (given to someone), so the preposition is missing.`,2:`'That' cannot follow a preposition; use 'to whom'.`,3:`The preposition 'to' already appears before 'whom', so do not repeat it.`},options:[`The scientist to whom the prize was given is only thirty.`,`The scientist whom the prize was given is only thirty.`,`The scientist to that the prize was given is only thirty.`,`The scientist to whom the prize was given to is only thirty.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-037`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'If only' + could expresses a wish about ability in the present: If only I could speak Spanish!`,wrongReasons:{0:`For an unreal present wish, 'can' moves back to 'could'.`,1:`Modal verbs like 'could' are followed by the base form without 'to'.`,2:`Two modals cannot go together; 'could' alone expresses ability.`},options:[`If only I can speak Spanish!`,`If only I could to speak Spanish!`,`If only I would can speak Spanish!`,`If only I could speak Spanish!`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-038`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Might have + past participle' expresses a possibility about the past: They might have taken the wrong road.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'might' use the base form, and for the past use 'might have taken'.`,1:`After 'have' use the past participle 'taken', not 'took'.`,3:`Do not put 'to' after the modal 'might'.`},options:[`They might took the wrong road.`,`They might have took the wrong road.`,`They might have taken the wrong road.`,`They might to have taken the wrong road.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-039`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In the active, 'make' + object is followed by the base form without 'to': They made us wait.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Active 'make' + object takes the base form without 'to'.`,2:`The -ing form is not used after 'make' + object.`,3:`The object comes directly after 'made', without 'to'.`},options:[`They made us to wait for two hours.`,`They made us wait for two hours.`,`They made us waiting for two hours.`,`They made to us wait for two hours.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-040`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use the past perfect to give the earlier cause of a past situation: I didn't recognise him because he had grown a beard.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present perfect does not match the past 'didn't recognise'; use 'had grown'.`,2:`The past participle of 'grow' is 'grown', not 'grew'.`,3:`'Was grown' is passive; he did the action, so use 'had grown'.`},options:[`I didn't recognise him because he had grown a beard.`,`I didn't recognise him because he has grown a beard.`,`I didn't recognise him because he had grew a beard.`,`I didn't recognise him because he was grown a beard.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-041`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The present simple is used for timetables and schedules: The train leaves at 7.15 tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{0:`With a singular subject the present simple needs -s: 'leaves'.`,1:`'Is' cannot be followed by the base form; use 'leaves' or 'is leaving'.`,2:`After 'will' use the base form 'leave'.`},options:[`The train leave at 7.15 tomorrow morning.`,`The train is leave at 7.15 tomorrow morning.`,`The train will leaving at 7.15 tomorrow morning.`,`The train leaves at 7.15 tomorrow morning.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-042`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'In spite of' can be followed by an -ing form: In spite of having little money, they travelled.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'In spite of' is followed by a noun or -ing form, not a clause.`,1:`The word 'of' is missing from 'in spite of'.`,3:`'Despite' does not take 'of'.`},options:[`In spite of they had little money, they travelled around the world.`,`In spite having little money, they travelled around the world.`,`In spite of having little money, they travelled around the world.`,`Despite of having little money, they travelled around the world.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-043`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In the third conditional the result clause is would have + past participle: it wouldn't have lost customers.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect 'has invested' cannot introduce an unreal past result; use 'had invested'.`,2:`'Won't have' does not express an imaginary past result; use 'wouldn't have'.`,3:`After a modal like 'would' use the base form 'have', not 'had'.`},options:[`If the company has invested in new technology, it wouldn't have lost so many customers.`,`If the company had invested in new technology, it wouldn't have lost so many customers.`,`If the company had invested in new technology, it won't have lost so many customers.`,`If the company had invested in new technology, it wouldn't had lost so many customers.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-044`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In reported speech 'will' usually becomes 'would' + base form: He said he would call me the next day.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Say' is not followed directly by a person; use 'He told me' or 'He said'.`,2:`'Told' needs an object such as 'me' after it.`,3:`After 'would' use the base form 'call', not the past form 'called'.`},options:[`He said he would call me the next day.`,`He said me he would call me the next day.`,`He told he would call me the next day.`,`He said he would called me the next day.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-045`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past modal passive is modal + have been + past participle: The documents should have been signed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Documents cannot sign anything, so the passive 'have been signed' is needed.`,1:`'Yesterday' is past, so you need 'should have been signed'.`,2:`After 'been' use the past participle 'signed'.`},options:[`The documents should have signed yesterday.`,`The documents should be signed yesterday.`,`The documents should have been sign yesterday.`,`The documents should have been signed yesterday.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-046`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`To comment on a whole clause, use ', which': He failed the test, which surprised everyone.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'That' cannot follow a comma to refer back to a whole idea.`,1:`'What' is not used to refer back to a previous clause; use 'which'.`,3:`'Who' refers to a person, but here the pronoun refers to the whole event.`},options:[`He failed the driving test, that surprised everyone.`,`He failed the driving test, what surprised everyone.`,`He failed the driving test, which surprised everyone.`,`He failed the driving test, who surprised everyone.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-047`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'If only' + past perfect expresses regret about the past: If only we had booked earlier.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect is not used after 'if only'; past regret needs 'had booked'.`,2:`After 'would' a past form is impossible, and past regret needs 'had booked'.`,3:`After 'had' use the past participle 'booked'.`},options:[`If only we have booked the tickets earlier.`,`If only we had booked the tickets earlier.`,`If only we would booked the tickets earlier.`,`If only we had book the tickets earlier.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-048`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Should have + past participle' expresses criticism or regret about the past: We should have left earlier.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Of' is a spelling mistake for the sound of 'have'; write 'should have'.`,2:`After 'have' you need the past participle 'left'.`,3:`After 'should' use the base form 'have', not 'had'.`},options:[`We should have left earlier.`,`We should of left earlier.`,`We should have leave earlier.`,`We should had left earlier.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-049`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Mind' is followed by the -ing form: Would you mind opening the window?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Mind' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + infinitive.`,1:`The base form cannot follow 'mind'; use 'opening'.`,2:`'If' must be followed by a clause, such as 'if I opened'.`},options:[`Would you mind to open the window?`,`Would you mind open the window?`,`Would you mind if opening the window?`,`Would you mind opening the window?`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-050`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'By the time' + past simple is often followed by the past perfect: By the time the police arrived, the thieves had escaped.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not fit a past story; use 'had escaped'.`,1:`After 'had' you need the past participle 'escaped'.`,3:`'Had arrive' is wrong; the earlier clause simply needs 'arrived'.`},options:[`By the time the police arrived, the thieves have escaped.`,`By the time the police arrived, the thieves had escape.`,`By the time the police arrived, the thieves had escaped.`,`By the time the police had arrive, the thieves had escaped.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-051`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In time clauses with 'as soon as', 'when' or 'until' we use the present simple for the future: I'll tell her as soon as she arrives.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Do not use 'will' in a time clause after 'as soon as'.`,2:`'Would' does not fit a real future time clause; use the present simple.`,3:`With 'she' the present simple needs -s: 'arrives'.`},options:[`I'll tell her as soon as she will arrive.`,`I'll tell her as soon as she arrives.`,`I'll tell her as soon as she would arrive.`,`I'll tell her as soon as she arrive.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-052`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'However' can start a new sentence and is followed by a comma: The roads were icy. However, the bus arrived on time.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Although' joins two clauses and cannot stand alone with a comma.`,2:`'Despite' needs a noun after it and cannot be used alone.`,3:`'Even though' introduces a clause; it cannot be followed by a comma like 'however'.`},options:[`The roads were icy. However, the bus arrived on time.`,`The roads were icy. Although, the bus arrived on time.`,`The roads were icy. Despite, the bus arrived on time.`,`The roads were icy. Even though, the bus arrived on time.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-053`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In the second conditional the if-clause uses the past simple, even when it comes second: I would call him if I had his number.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The if-clause should contain the past simple 'had', not 'would have'.`,1:`'Will' does not match a past simple if-clause; an unreal situation needs 'would'.`,2:`The present perfect 'have had' is not used in a second conditional if-clause.`},options:[`I would call him if I would have his number.`,`I will call him if I had his number.`,`I would call him if I have had his number.`,`I would call him if I had his number.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-054`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Suggest' is followed by an -ing form or by a that-clause, not by an infinitive: Maria suggested going to the museum.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Suggest' is not followed by 'to' + infinitive; use '-ing' or 'that we go'.`,1:`'Suggest' does not take the pattern object + infinitive; say 'suggested that we go'.`,3:`After 'that' you need a full verb form such as 'we go' or 'we should go'.`},options:[`Maria suggested to go to the new museum.`,`Maria suggested us to go to the new museum.`,`Maria suggested going to the new museum.`,`Maria suggested that we going to the new museum.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-055`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'It is believed that...' is an impersonal passive used for general beliefs: It is believed that the painting was stolen.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'It' cannot believe anything; the impersonal structure is passive: 'It is believed'.`,2:`The painting did not steal anything; it 'was stolen'.`,3:`With 'It is believed' use a that-clause; the infinitive pattern needs 'The painting is believed to...'.`},options:[`It believes that the painting was stolen in 1990.`,`It is believed that the painting was stolen in 1990.`,`It is believed that the painting stole in 1990.`,`It is believed the painting to have been stolen in 1990.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-056`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`When the relative pronoun is the object, it can be left out: The film we watched last night was moving.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The relative clause already refers to the film, so do not add 'it'.`,2:`'What' cannot follow a noun as a relative pronoun; use 'that', 'which' or nothing.`,3:`'Who' is only for people, not for films.`},options:[`The film we watched last night was really moving.`,`The film we watched it last night was really moving.`,`The film what we watched last night was really moving.`,`The film who we watched last night was really moving.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-057`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After 'wish', 'were' is used for all persons in careful English: I wish I were taller.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'wish' the present 'am' changes to the past 'were'.`,1:`'Wish' does not take 'will'; use the past form 'were'.`,2:`'Been' needs an auxiliary; for the present use 'were', for the past 'had been'.`},options:[`I wish I am taller.`,`I wish I will be taller.`,`I wish I been taller.`,`I wish I were taller.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-058`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence correctly says that wearing a suit is not necessary?`,explanation:`'Don't have to' means something is not necessary, while 'mustn't' means it is forbidden: You don't have to wear a suit.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Mustn't' means it is forbidden, not that it is unnecessary.`,1:`'Must' does not form negatives with 'don't'.`,3:`The modal 'needn't' is followed by the base form without 'to'.`},options:[`You mustn't wear a suit to the party.`,`You don't must wear a suit to the party.`,`You don't have to wear a suit to the party.`,`You needn't to wear a suit to the party.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-059`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Let' + object is followed by the base form without 'to': My parents let me stay out late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Let' + object takes the base form without 'to'.`,2:`The -ing form is not used after 'let' + object.`,3:`'Allow' + object needs 'to': 'allowed me to stay'.`},options:[`My parents let me to stay out late at weekends.`,`My parents let me stay out late at weekends.`,`My parents let me staying out late at weekends.`,`My parents allowed me stay out late at weekends.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-060`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous explains the cause of a past result: She was tired because she had been working all day.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present perfect continuous does not match the past 'was tired'.`,2:`After 'had been' use the -ing form 'working'.`,3:`The continuous form needs 'been': 'had been working'.`},options:[`She was tired because she had been working all day.`,`She was tired because she has been working all day.`,`She was tired because she had been work all day.`,`She was tired because she had working all day.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-061`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The present continuous is used for fixed future arrangements: We're meeting the clients on Thursday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous needs the auxiliary 'are'.`,1:`After 'are' use the -ing form 'meeting'.`,2:`After 'will' use the base form or 'be meeting'.`},options:[`We meeting the clients on Thursday afternoon.`,`We're meet the clients on Thursday afternoon.`,`We'll meeting the clients on Thursday afternoon.`,`We're meeting the clients on Thursday afternoon.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-062`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'In case' is followed by the present simple to talk about a possible future event: Take a map in case you get lost.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Do not use 'will' after 'in case'; use the present simple.`,1:`'In case of' is followed by a noun; before a clause use 'in case'.`,3:`'Would' does not fit a real precaution about the future; use 'get'.`},options:[`Take a map in case you will get lost.`,`Take a map in case of you get lost.`,`Take a map in case you get lost.`,`Take a map in case you would get lost.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-063`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Provided that' works like 'if' and takes the present simple for future conditions: Provided that you return it, you can borrow it.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Like 'if', 'provided that' is followed by the present simple, not 'will'.`,2:`'Would' does not belong in a real condition introduced by 'provided that'.`,3:`The past 'returned' does not match the real future result 'you can borrow it'.`},options:[`Provided that you will return the car by Friday, you can borrow it.`,`Provided that you return the car by Friday, you can borrow it.`,`Provided that you would return the car by Friday, you can borrow it.`,`Provided that you returned the car by Friday, you can borrow it.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-064`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Indirect questions with 'whether' keep statement word order and often move the tense back: whether the flight had been cancelled.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Indirect questions do not invert the subject and verb after 'whether'.`,2:`Keep the subject before the verb in an indirect question: 'whether the flight was'.`,3:`A flight is cancelled by someone, so the passive 'had been cancelled' is needed.`},options:[`They wanted to know whether the flight had been cancelled.`,`They wanted to know whether had the flight been cancelled.`,`They wanted to know whether was the flight cancelled.`,`They wanted to know whether the flight had cancelled.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-065`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past simple passive is was/were + past participle, and the doer is introduced by 'by': It was opened by the mayor.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without 'was' this is not a complete passive sentence.`,1:`The hospital did not do the opening, so use the past participle 'opened'.`,2:`In the passive the person who does the action is introduced by 'by', not 'from'.`},options:[`The new hospital opened by the mayor in May.`,`The new hospital was opening by the mayor in May.`,`The new hospital was opened from the mayor in May.`,`The new hospital was opened by the mayor in May.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-066`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After 'everything', 'anything' and 'nothing' we use 'that' (or no pronoun), not 'what': Everything that she said was true.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'What' already means 'the thing that', so it cannot follow 'everything'.`,1:`The pronoun 'it' repeats the object, which the relative pronoun already replaces.`,3:`'Who' is for people; 'everything' refers to things.`},options:[`Everything what she said was true.`,`Everything which she said it was true.`,`Everything that she said was true.`,`Everything who she said was true.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-067`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With 'wish', 'can' becomes 'could' + base form: He wishes he could find a better job.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'wish', 'can' moves back to 'could'.`,2:`After 'could' use the base form 'find', without -s.`,3:`Do not put 'to' after the modal 'could'.`},options:[`He wishes he can find a better job.`,`He wishes he could find a better job.`,`He wishes he could finds a better job.`,`He wishes he could to find a better job.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-068`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'May not have + past participle' says it is possible that something did not happen: She may not have received my email.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'may not' you need 'have' + past participle for a past possibility.`,2:`The negative goes after the modal: 'may not have', not 'may haven't'.`,3:`After a modal use the base form 'have', not 'has'.`},options:[`She may not have received my email yet.`,`She may not received my email yet.`,`She may haven't received my email yet.`,`She may not has received my email yet.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-069`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After a preposition we use the -ing form: She is interested in learning photography.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The adjective 'interested' goes with the preposition 'in'.`,1:`After the preposition 'in' use the -ing form 'learning'.`,2:`The correct preposition is 'in', not 'on'.`},options:[`She is interested to learning photography.`,`She is interested in learn photography.`,`She is interested on learning photography.`,`She is interested in learning photography.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-070`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After 'as soon as' the past perfect can show that one action was completed before the next: As soon as we had finished lunch, we went for a walk.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not match the past simple 'went'.`,1:`After 'had' use the past participle 'finished'.`,3:`The second action is the main event, so use the past simple 'went'.`},options:[`As soon as we have finished lunch, we went for a walk.`,`As soon as we had finish lunch, we went for a walk.`,`As soon as we had finished lunch, we went for a walk.`,`As soon as we had finished lunch, we had go for a walk.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-071`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Be about to' + base form means something will happen very soon: She is about to start her new job.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The expression is 'be about to' + base form, not the -ing form.`,2:`'To' is needed in 'be about to'.`,3:`After 'to' use the base form 'start'.`},options:[`She is about starting her new job.`,`She is about to start her new job.`,`She is about start her new job.`,`She is about to starting her new job.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-072`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Even though' introduces a strong contrast and is followed by a clause: He didn't get the job, even though he had excellent qualifications.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Even' alone cannot join two clauses; use 'even though'.`,2:`'Despite' needs a noun or -ing form, e.g. 'despite having'.`,3:`'Even though' is followed by a clause, not 'of' + noun.`},options:[`He didn't get the job, even though he had excellent qualifications.`,`He didn't get the job, even he had excellent qualifications.`,`He didn't get the job, despite he had excellent qualifications.`,`He didn't get the job, even though of his excellent qualifications.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-073`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`To give advice we often say 'If I were you, I would...': If I were you, I would apologise.`,wrongReasons:{0:`This situation is imaginary, so use the past form 'were', not 'am'.`,1:`An imaginary situation needs 'would' in the result clause, not 'will'.`,2:`The result clause needs 'would' + base form: 'I would apologise'.`},options:[`If I am you, I would apologise to her.`,`If I was you, I will apologise to her.`,`If I were you, I apologise to her.`,`If I were you, I would apologise to her.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-074`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Admit' is followed by an -ing form or by a that-clause: He admitted breaking the window.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Admit' is not followed by 'to' + base form; use 'admitted breaking' or 'admitted to breaking'.`,1:`'Admit' does not take a person as direct object; say 'admitted to me' or just 'admitted'.`,3:`The base form cannot follow 'admit'; use the -ing form 'breaking'.`},options:[`He admitted to break the window.`,`He admitted me that he broke the window.`,`He admitted breaking the window.`,`He admitted break the window.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-075`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The present perfect passive is has/have + been + past participle: My phone has been stolen.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The phone did not steal anything; you need the passive 'has been stolen'.`,2:`After 'been' you need the past participle 'stolen'.`,3:`'Been' follows 'has/have', not 'is'.`},options:[`My phone has stolen!`,`My phone has been stolen!`,`My phone has been steal!`,`My phone is been stolen!`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-076`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After 'the reason' we use 'why' or 'that' to introduce the relative clause: The reason why she left is a mystery.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Because' is not a relative pronoun; after 'the reason' use 'why' or 'that'.`,2:`'Which' does not work here without a preposition; use 'why'.`,3:`'Where' refers to places, not to reasons.`},options:[`The reason why she left is still a mystery.`,`The reason because she left is still a mystery.`,`The reason which she left is still a mystery.`,`The reason where she left is still a mystery.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-077`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Wish + would' is used for things we want to change but cannot control, like the weather: I wish it would stop raining.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Wish' is not followed by the present simple; use 'would stop'.`,1:`'Will' does not follow 'wish'; use 'would' or say 'I hope it stops'.`,2:`'Would' must be followed by the base form 'stop'.`},options:[`I wish it stops raining.`,`I wish it will stop raining.`,`I wish it would stopped raining.`,`I wish it would stop raining.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-078`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Had better' is followed by the base form without 'to': You had better take an umbrella.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Had better' is followed by the base form without 'to'.`,1:`The -ing form cannot follow 'had better'.`,3:`The expression is fixed: 'had better', not 'have better'.`},options:[`You had better to take an umbrella.`,`You had better taking an umbrella.`,`You had better take an umbrella.`,`You have better take an umbrella.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-079`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Decide' takes 'to' + infinitive, and the negative is 'decide not to': We decided not to go.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'to' use the base form 'go', and put 'not' before 'to'.`,2:`'Decide' is followed by 'to' + infinitive, not the -ing form.`,3:`Do not use 'don't' after 'decide'; the negative is 'not to go'.`},options:[`We decided to not going to the concert.`,`We decided not to go to the concert.`,`We decided not going to the concert.`,`We decided don't go to the concert.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-080`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After 'It was the first time' we use the past perfect: It was the first time I had ever seen snow.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'It was the first time', the present perfect changes to the past perfect.`,2:`After 'had' use the past participle 'seen', not 'saw'.`,3:`This expression needs the past perfect 'had seen', not the past continuous.`},options:[`It was the first time I had ever seen snow.`,`It was the first time I have ever seen snow.`,`It was the first time I had ever saw snow.`,`It was the first time I was ever seeing snow.`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-081`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`We use 'will' for promises and offers made at the moment of speaking: I promise I'll help you later.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'will' use the base form 'help'.`,1:`'Going' must be followed by 'to': 'going to help'.`,2:`Do not put 'to' after 'will'.`},options:[`I promise I'll helping you with your homework later.`,`I promise I'm going help you with your homework later.`,`I promise I will to help you with your homework later.`,`I promise I'll help you with your homework later.`],correct:3},{id:`b2-sc-082`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Whereas' contrasts two facts and joins them by itself: Whereas my brother loves sport, I prefer reading.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Whereas' already joins the clauses, so 'but' is extra.`,1:`'Despite' cannot be followed by a full clause.`,3:`'Whereas' is followed by a clause, not 'of' + noun.`},options:[`Whereas my brother loves sport, but I prefer reading.`,`Despite my brother loves sport, I prefer reading.`,`Whereas my brother loves sport, I prefer reading.`,`Whereas of my brother loving sport, I prefer reading.`],correct:2},{id:`b2-sc-083`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Phrasal verbs keep their particle in the passive: The children are being looked after by their grandparents.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The phrasal verb is 'look after', so 'after' must stay in the passive.`,2:`The children receive the care, so the verb must be passive: 'are being looked after'.`,3:`After 'are' you need 'being' for the continuous passive, not 'be'.`},options:[`The children are being looked by their grandparents.`,`The children are being looked after by their grandparents.`,`The children are looking after by their grandparents.`,`The children are be looked after by their grandparents.`],correct:1},{id:`b2-sc-084`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use 'who' for people in defining relative clauses, followed by the base form after a modal: anyone who can speak Japanese.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Which' is for things; 'anyone' is a person, so use 'who'.`,2:`After the modal 'can' the verb stays in the base form 'speak'.`,3:`'Whose' shows possession and must be followed by a noun.`},options:[`Is there anyone here who can speak Japanese?`,`Is there anyone here which can speak Japanese?`,`Is there anyone here who can speaks Japanese?`,`Is there anyone here whose can speak Japanese?`],correct:0},{id:`b2-sc-085`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`B2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`'Due to' is followed by a noun phrase and gives the reason: The museum was closed due to a power cut.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The expression is 'due to'; the word 'to' is missing.`,1:`'Due to' needs a noun phrase, not a full clause.`,2:`'Because' needs a clause; before a noun use 'because of'.`},options:[`The museum was closed due a power cut.`,`The museum was closed due to there was a power cut.`,`The museum was closed because a power cut.`,`The museum was closed due to a power cut.`],correct:3}]};export{e as default};